- op: discuss  # 79a6
  at: '2026-02-18T13:44:37Z'  # 79a6
  by: agent  # 79a6
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 79a6
  clock: 5  # 79a6
  nonce: 79a6  # 79a6
  message: "Assembly analyzer scope: Start with x86/x86-64 AT&T and Intel syntax. Detect labels as symbols, call/jmp targets as call edges. Could use tree-sitter-asm if available, otherwise regex-based parsing of common directives (.global, .text, .data)."  # 79a6
- op: discuss  # 077e
  at: '2026-02-18T14:01:13Z'  # 077e
  by: agent  # 077e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 077e
  clock: 6  # 077e
  nonce: 077e  # 077e
  message: "Priority consideration: Assembly files are rare in most repos but critical in OS kernels, bootloaders, and embedded projects. Consider starting with ELF-targeted x86-64 since that covers Linux kernel and most open-source assembly usage."  # 077e
- op: discuss  # 0d3b
  at: '2026-02-18T14:41:45Z'  # 0d3b
  by: agent  # 0d3b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0d3b
  clock: 7  # 0d3b
  nonce: 0d3b  # 0d3b
  message: "ARM assembly should also be on the roadmap given the prevalence of ARM in mobile and embedded. ARM syntax is sufficiently different from x86 that it would need its own detection logic."  # 0d3b
- op: discuss  # eed8
  at: '2026-02-18T14:55:15Z'  # eed8
  by: agent  # eed8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # eed8
  clock: 8  # eed8
  nonce: eed8  # eed8
  message: "RISC-V assembly is also worth considering given the growing adoption. The instruction set is clean and regular which should make parsing straightforward compared to x86 with its legacy prefixes and variable-length encoding."  # eed8
